========================================================================================
GATE -- the wired Ewald kernel, dz = 0 and dz != 0
  M = 4, pitch = 0.5 m, omega = 60.0 rad/s
========================================================================================

  [E1] dz != 0: the EWALD route vs the SPECTRAL route (what is used)
       Two constructions sharing no code -- real-space Ewald with
       Kupradze derivatives, against a reciprocal sum of the spectral
       9x9. Compared away from k_par = 0, where both are well
       conditioned; AT k_par = 0 the Ewald route loses (eta/kappa)^n
       and is shown separately as the reason it is not used here.
        dz (m)   Bloch pt          G          C          H          S
          0.50      (0,0)   4.74e-11   4.44e-07   2.22e-07   1.04e-05
          0.50      (1,0)   1.92e-10   3.29e-10   3.96e-10   8.18e-10
          0.50      (2,3)   1.65e-11   7.33e-11   7.33e-11   9.10e-11
          1.00      (0,0)   2.99e-11   1.05e-06   5.23e-07   5.97e-03
          1.00      (1,0)   3.90e-10   7.23e-09   4.21e-09   4.56e-08
          1.00      (2,3)   8.26e-11   6.87e-11   8.39e-11   5.04e-09
       At k_par = 0 the Ewald S block departs by 6.0e-03 and grows with
       dz -- the direction a refinement ladder runs. Hence the split.

  [E1b] the spectral route vs gate_interplane_bloch_sum's own reference
       dz = 0.50  (0,0):  5.82e-18
       dz = 0.50  (2,3):  8.70e-16
       dz = 1.00  (0,0):  5.82e-18
       dz = 1.00  (2,3):  1.83e-16

  [E2] eta-independence by block
        dz (m)                     G          C          H          S
          0.00              1.10e-16   3.43e-15   2.58e-15   5.74e-13
          0.50              0.00e+00   0.00e+00   0.00e+00   0.00e+00
       kappa_P d = 0.0060; the S block loses about
       (eta/kappa)^4 in the fourth-derivative term. [E5] decides if it matters.

  [E3] cutoff convergence
       dz =  0.00 m   cutoff 4 -> 7:  4.82e-31
       dz =  0.50 m   cutoff 4 -> 7:  9.07e-16

  [E4] the R = 0 convention (included at dz != 0, excluded at dz = 0)
       the R = 0 term is 1.3% of the dz = 0.5 m kernel at k = 0.
       If it were wrongly excluded the inter-plane coupling would lose
       its largest single contribution, and no same-plane test would see it.

  [E5] end-to-end: the computed reflection coefficient vs eta
        eta/eta0  cutoff            floor         rel
            1.00       4 1.4290303770e-03    0.00e+00
            0.40      16 1.4290303770e-03    8.91e-13
            1.40       8 1.4290303770e-03    3.03e-12
            2.00      10 1.4290303771e-03    6.06e-11
       A noisy kernel block only matters if the noise arrives here.

========================================================================================
  [E1]  Ewald vs spectral   G/C/H: 7.23e-09   S: 4.56e-08
  [E1b] spectral vs gated reference: 8.70e-16
  [E2]  eta-independence    G/C/H: 3.43e-15   S: 5.74e-13
  [E3]  cutoff convergence       : 9.07e-16
  [E5]  end-to-end eta stability : 6.06e-11

  PASS: both branches of the wired kernel are correct. The dz != 0
  branch agrees with a spectral construction that shares no code with
  it, so the R = 0 convention and the Bloch phase sign are confirmed
  against something independent rather than against themselves.

  dz != 0 uses the SPECTRAL sum and dz = 0 the Ewald sum, because
  Ewald is needed only where the plain reciprocal sum diverges, and
  at dz != 0 it is the worse-conditioned of the two. The residual
  S-block eta-dependence at dz = 0 is 5.7e-13, and [E5] shows it does
  not reach the observable: a 5x change in eta moves the answer by 6.1e-11.
  A case relying on the strain-strain coupling directly should
  re-check [E5] rather than assume this carries over.
========================================================================================
